      "knowledge_point": "Calculating the Slope of a Normal Line to a Curve at a Point",
      "problem": "Find the slope of the normal line to the curve $y={x}^{2}+x$ at the point $(1,2).$",
      "worked_solution": "Let $f(x)={x}^{2}+x.$ First, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}+x) \\\\  & =2x+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the slope of the tangent at the point $(1,2):$\n\n$$\nm={f}^{′}(1)=2(1)+1=3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line ${m}^{′}$ is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$",
