      "math_academy_id": "q-47410",
      "knowledge_point_id": "55a323ba-f2a7-5a49-abd4-a9812518ef8c",
      "knowledge_point": "Determining the Equation of a Normal Line to a Curve at a Point",
      "problem": "The equation of the normal line to the curve $y=3{x}^{2}+4x$ at the point $(1,7)$ is given by $x+py=q$ where $p$ and $q$ are constants. Find the value of $p+q.$",
      "worked_solution": "Let $f(x)=3{x}^{2}+4x.$ First, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{x}^{2}+4x)=6x+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the slope of the tangent at the point $(1,7):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={f}^{′}(1)=6(1)+4=10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line ${m}^{′}$ is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{10}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(7) & =-\\frac{1}{10}(x-1) \\\\ 10y-70 & =-x+1 \\\\ x+10y & =71.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p+q=10+71=81.$",
